# What PuralvOS deliberately does not ship
#
# Format: one package or glob per line, "#" starts a comment, blank lines are
# ignored. This file is applied AFTER the tasks in tasks.list.
#
# Read this as a list of decisions, not a list of rubbish. Each entry says why
# it is here, because a removal with no stated reason is one that will get
# reverted by somebody who cannot tell what it was for.
#
# The single most important structural choice is what is NOT in tasks.list:
# ubuntustudio-desktop is never installed. It is what drags in LibreOffice,
# snapd, synaptic, printer drivers and avahi. Excluding those one at a time
# afterwards is a losing game against its Recommends list, so PuralvOS simply
# does not ask for it. Everything below is what leaks in through other routes.

# --- kernel ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only one kernel boots a PuralvOS machine: the real time one. The general
# kernel comes in as a dependency of the desktop metapackages and as the
# default on the installation media, and leaving both installed means the
# machine might boot the wrong one after a routine upgrade, which for an audio
# workstation is a latency regression nobody would notice until a show.
#
# The order matters. linux-lowlatency is installed in tasks.list FIRST, then
# the general kernel is removed. Removing first would let apt decide to keep
# the lowlatency kernel only as a recommendation.

linux-generic
linux-generic-hwe-26.10
linux-generic-hwe-26.10-edge
linux-image-generic
linux-image-generic-hwe-26.10
linux-image-generic-hwe-26.10-edge
linux-headers-generic
linux-headers-generic-hwe-26.10
linux-headers-generic-hwe-26.10-edge

# --- general purpose desktop ----------------------------------------------
  #
  # LibreOffice STAYS. It was on this list and it was wrong: the reason given
  # was "a document suite is not an audio or video tool", which is a statement
  # about taste, not about whether the two people who use this machine need it.
  # Alv and Puria write things. A workstation that cannot open a .odt or a
  # .docx is a workstation with a papercut in it every single day.
  #
  # Recorded because the next person to read this file will assume every
  # general purpose package here is excluded, and will "helpfully" add it back
  # or take it out. Neither. It is present, on purpose.
  #
  # Note what this means for the size argument: 21 packages of LibreOffice is
  # not a problem worth solving. Snap was 4 GB. The trim is not about making
  # the image small, it is about removing what is actively in the way.

# --- packaging and app distribution ---------------------------------------
  #
  # NO SNAP. Not a leaning, a decision, and not one to be revisited per package.
  #
  # snapd is a second package manager with its own daemon, its own store, its
  # own update schedule and its own opinions about what "installed" means. On
  # an image whose entire promise is "these packages are the ones we test and
  # upgrade", a parallel system nobody audits is a liability.
  #
  # The stock Ubuntu Studio live image carries 4 GB of it: 14 snaps and 2 GB of
  # .snap payloads under /var/lib/snapd. That is more than the whole 69-package
  # trim saves, so this is the largest single saving available in the build.
  #
  # The snaps it carries are firefox, thunderbird, gnome-46-2404, core24,
  # firmware-updater, desktop-security-center, prompting-client,
  # snapd-desktop-integration, ubuntu-desktop-bootstrap and several theme and
  # runtime bases.
  #
  # CONSEQUENCE, stated now rather than discovered later: on the stock image
  # Firefox and Thunderbird ARE snaps. Excluding snapd removes them, and
  # purging the `firefox` deb does not help either, because in the archive
  # that package is a transitional wrapper whose only job is to install the
  # snap. If Firefox is wanted it has to come from Mozilla's own apt
  # repository, which is a real repository with real signing and is not the
  # same thing as a snap. Decide that before an image is built, not after
  # somebody notices the browser has vanished.
  #
  # plasma-discover-backend-snap is only a Recommends of plasma-discover, so
  # building with --no-install-recommends drops it without a fight.
  #
  # The apt sources are pinned to the Ubuntu archive in apt/puralvos.sources.
  # Anything not in it does not get into a PuralvOS image by accident.

  snapd
  snapd-desktop-integration
  plasma-discover-backend-snap
  firefox
  thunderbird

# synaptic duplicates apt and gets no package pinning, which is exactly the
# behaviour PuralvOS has to avoid: an unpinned upgrade tool would happily
# pull a new kernel past the curated set.
synaptic

# --- hardware nobody doing media needs ------------------------------------
#
# Printer drivers, firmware updaters and PCMCIA/CardBus support come from the
# general desktop. A workstation that only does audio and video does not need
# to know what a LaserJet 4000 is.

hplip
printer-driver-brlaser
printer-driver-c2esp
printer-driver-foo2zjs
printer-driver-m2300w
printer-driver-min12xxw
printer-driver-ptouch
printer-driver-pxljr
printer-driver-sag-gdi
printer-driver-splix
cups-filters

# firmware updates touch the flash chip of every device in the machine. On a
# live image that is a genuinely bad idea, and on an installed one it is an
# update the user should trigger deliberately, not something an image
# refresh does behind their back.
fwupd
fwupd-signed

pcmciautils
laptop-detect

# --- diagnostics that only matter when something is already broken --------

memtest86+

# --- games -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NO GAMES. This is a statement about the product, not a leaning.
#
# There is no games section in tasks.list, none of the three discipline
# metapackages is a game, and the only thing in the base image that suggests
# otherwise is an EMPTY /usr/games directory that dpkg creates for its own
# reasons. That directory is empty on the built image and nothing in this
# product puts anything in it.
#
# It is recorded here because the empty directory is exactly the kind of thing
# that gets "cleaned up" by the next person who finds it, and because the
# desktop-entry search path in tools/lib/desktop-exec.sh used to include
# /usr/games. It does not now, and tools/test-desktop-exec asserts that it does
# not: a search path containing a directory belonging to a class of software
# this product does not ship is an assumption about the product.
#
# Related, and for the same reason, the education metapackage stays out:
# ubuntu-edu-music brings a classroom's worth of notation, ear training and
# music theory tools. It is not in tasks.list, and the self test's curated
# launch list mirrors tasks.list rather than listing them and skipping.
ubuntu-edu-music

# --- puredata (Pd) ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Pure Data is a visual programming language for multimedia. While it is an
# audio tool, it is not required for the core audio, video, and graphics
# workstation use case. Users who need it can install it separately.
puredata
puredata-data
puredata-doc
puredata-utils
